The design doc is written at `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md` in the `linker-dht` worktree, with cross-references added to the three related docs. Nothing is staged or committed, and no code changed.

## What the contract fixes

- **Content source.** The DHT node's local store is the linker's content source. A module's closure is loaded onto the node first, and the node stays the only step owner.
- **Closure walker.** An explicit walker answers missing, complete or invalid as data. It always covers the tree, all three lowered images and transitive requires, whichever format a request names.
- **Staged load.** The index loader is generalized into a load over any walk. Failure reasons become data carrying the DHT's miss cause, replacing S5's strings.
- **Rows every round.** The indexer materializes each evaluated program's rows, per the owner's decision. The publish banner must say the code itself is shared.
- **Names.** Signed envelopes in explicitly loaded index snapshots resolve names. The doc fixes the envelope, proof, signed bytes with a domain prefix, hex key encoding, the key-as-principal id and the cross-host vectors.
- **Key file.** A stable key is loaded from a versioned file and never minted silently. The sequence is derived from the publisher's own index. Loss, replacement, a lost index directory and disclosure each have a stated consequence and remedy.
- **Consensus.** The same address from several declared principals resolves. Different addresses refuse as ambiguous.
- **Re-check.** The ticker re-checks only when a load that a pending link waits on completes or fails.
- **Budget is not a lease.** A request budget bounds one fetch, never the pending run.
- **API and slices.** The plain Clojure API is listed with an interface gate forbidding a second loader in the REPL. Slices L0 to L5 carry files and the tightened acceptance, followed by a deferrals section.

## Things I added beyond the inputs

These came out of reading the tree while consolidating. Each is worth a look from the second reviewer.

- **Row writes will hit the DHT's pending-write bound.** The bound is 64, and rows every round means many puts per round. I specified a replicate backlog in `dao.space.dht` so a large program is never reported busy. This is new work in L4.
- **Republishing a name needs a retraction.** The fold has no recency rule, so one principal asserting a name twice with different manifests is ambiguous. Publish therefore writes a retraction and the new assertion together.
- **Inter-definition modules link on two VMs only.** A module whose export reads another module-level definition links on the stack and register VMs today. The doc leaves that linker rule alone, makes the publisher report per-format link outcomes, and scopes the four-VM gate to the closed corpus.
- **Transitive requires still resolve by name.** The reader's name environment must resolve each dependency to the pinned address, so a publisher asserts its dependencies in the same index.
- **A canonical encoder change invalidates every signature.** The doc states this and promises no migration.
- **Dart needs a synchronous Ed25519 package.** The fold verifies synchronously, so an async-only package is ruled out. The doc leaves the package choice and pin to L0.

## Open item for the owner or the L4 brief

The doc does not specify how a derived module's footprint is computed. It defers to the dependency-completion doc and tells the engineer to stop and report if that computation is unavailable, with no empty default.
